## Gate Decisions and Acceptance

### G1 — Reporting allowlist and privacy

- API payloads use an explicit schema-versioned allowlist; unknown fields fail closed.
- Project and instance identity are separate; local SQLite remains authoritative.
- Idempotency keys, monotonic sequence numbers, and counter epochs prevent duplicate or misleading joins.
- Unknown, partial, stale, reset, or incompatible telemetry is represented explicitly.
- Prompts, responses, source, paths, commands, credentials, raw diagnostics, and heartbeat history are prohibited.

### G2 — Human authentication and instance enrollment

- Production requires configured OIDC or a maintained MFA-capable Django authentication backend;
  deployment refuses an unconfigured authentication mode.
- Human sessions and reporter tokens are separate. Pending enrollment is untrusted, expires after
  ten minutes, is rate/cap bounded, and requires authenticated approval of the matching code.
- Reporter tokens are opaque 256-bit values, verifier-only server-side, protected user-local on the
  client, immediately revocable, rotatable, and replaced through re-pairing.

### G3 — Fleet, platform, and reporter operations

- First release qualifies Linux directly and portable locking/scheduling behavior through Linux,
  macOS, and Windows tests; unsupported scheduler integration remains explicit.
- The acceptance load is 100 reporting instances and 25 concurrent active reporters without queue loss.
- The safety pass defaults to every 15 minutes and is configurable within bounded limits.
- One-second outbox polling, one-minute active heartbeats, and a two-hour empty-outbox idle exit are fixed.

### G4 — Material-event and failure retention

- Material state changes default to 90 days; sanitized App Server failure groups default to 30 days
  and at most 100 bounded occurrences per group. Both are configurable and deletion is auditable.
- Current presence and `last_seen` update in place. One-minute heartbeat history is never retained.
- Work Efficiency windows preserve counter epochs and coverage; missing usage is unknown, never zero.

### G5 — Public presentation and dashboard accessibility

- Existing public routes, headers, footers, typography, colors, spacing, responsive behavior,
  documentation navigation, and static delivery remain compatibility baselines.
- Dashboard tabs are stable routes with reload/history/direct-link behavior and keyboard/focus tests.
- One tab level, a collapsible desktop navigation pane, narrow-screen drawer, and visible attention
  summaries are required; no full SPA is introduced.

### G6 — Production recovery, release, and reconciliation

- PostgreSQL receives encrypted daily backups with seven daily and four weekly restore points.
- Acceptance requires a verified restore into an isolated database, RPO at most 24 hours, RTO at
  most two hours, and independently tested application rollback with backward-compatible migrations.
- A disconnected installed client must enroll, report, survive retry/restart, and be revocable.
- Exact-SHA validation, release provenance, production routes/health, public compatibility, and
  terminal reconciled Campaign → PRM → Map → Idea outcomes are mandatory.

